WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.542
Semi-structured data has some organization without requiring every record to look exactly the same.

00:00:06.542 --> 00:00:09.202
JSON is a common example.

00:00:09.202 --> 00:00:15.822
Fields have names, but values can be nested, and different records may contain different keys.

00:00:15.822 --> 00:00:22.967
That flexibility is useful for APIs, devices, logs, and fast-changing applications.

00:00:22.967 --> 00:00:26.877
The tradeoff is that analysts often need to flatten records, handle optional fields,

00:00:26.877 --> 00:00:31.772
and manage schema changes before analysis.

00:00:31.772 --> 00:00:39.057
A useful project question is: what preparation step is required before analysis can even begin?

00:00:39.057 --> 00:00:42.307
A relational table may be ready for profiling, JSON may need flattening,

00:00:42.307 --> 00:00:46.722
and free text may need NLP or another extraction step.

00:00:46.722 --> 00:00:53.862
The form of the data changes the engineering path, but it does not by itself tell us whether

00:00:53.862 --> 00:00:55.920
the data is high quality.
